  What was wrong

  The device token had only the operator.admin scope, but newer versions of the CLI request additional scopes
  (operator.approvals, operator.pairing, and operator.read) on connection. The gateway rejected these as unauthorized
  scope upgrades, producing the pairing required (1008) error.

  What was fixed

  1. Updated ~/.openclaw/devices/paired.json to add the missing scopes to unblock the initial CLI connection
  2. Used openclaw devices rotate (via the now-working connection) to properly issue a new token with all four scopes

  ---
  Commands to use if this happens again

  Diagnose:
  openclaw doctor
  journalctl --user -u openclaw-gateway --no-pager -n 30

  Look for scope-upgrade entries in the journal — they'll tell you exactly which scopes are missing (scopesFrom vs
  scopesTo).

  Fix (full sequence):
  # 1. Get your device ID
  DEVICE_ID=$(python3 -c "import json; d=json.load(open('/home/zoe/.openclaw/identity/device.json'));
  print(d['deviceId'])")

  # 2. Bootstrap the connection by temporarily adding the needed scopes to paired.json,
  #    then rotate via the API to make it permanent:
  openclaw devices rotate \
    --device "$DEVICE_ID" \
    --role operator \
    --scope operator.admin \
    --scope operator.approvals \
    --scope operator.pairing \
    --scope operator.read \
    --json

  # 3. Verify
  openclaw health

  If the CLI can't connect at all (chicken-and-egg — rotate itself fails), add scopes to ~/.openclaw/devices/paired.json
   manually first, then kill and let systemd restart the gateway, then run the rotate command:
  # Kill gateway so systemd restarts it with the edited paired.json
  kill $(pgrep -f openclaw-gateway)
  sleep 5
  # Then run the rotate command above
